\section{Elementary finite geometry and separation}\label{sec:foundations}
Ordinary real completeness, finite-dimensional linear algebra, compactness of closed bounded sets, and the elementary properties of convex secants are the background foundations. Here we provide the additional finite convex and polyhedral arguments used, without an unavailable source theorem.

\begin{lemma}[Feasible rational density]\label{lem:density}
Every point of a nonempty rational polyhedron is a limit of rational feasible points in its minimal face. A continuous printed-PC function satisfies every real-endpoint PCC with the same rational face-incidence constraints.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
At a given point collect the rational equations and currently active inequalities as equalities. Gaussian elimination gives a rational particular solution and a rational basis of the homogeneous solution space. Approximate its real coordinates by rational ones. The finitely many inactive inequalities have strictly positive slack and remain satisfied. This proves density, including boundary and lower-dimensional faces.

For the PCC claim discard zero weights, write each endpoint cell as $A_jx_j\geq b_j$ with equations included, and put $t_j=\alpha_jx_j$. The constraints
\[
 \alpha_j\geq0,\quad\sum\alpha_j=1,\quad A_jt_j\geq b_j\alpha_j,\quad\sum t_j\in\tau
\]
are rational linear constraints on $(\alpha_j,t_j)$. Rational feasible approximants have positive $\alpha_j$ eventually. Their endpoints $t_j/\alpha_j$ and centers $\sum t_j$ are rational and stay in their assigned cells and center face. Apply the printed inequalities and take limits by continuity. No new carrier or coarsening is used.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Finite hull and cone elimination]\label{lem:hull}
In a real space of dimension $a$, the convex hull of a compact set $K$ is compact. A cone generated by a finite list is closed. A pointed rational polyhedron is the hull of finitely many rational vertices plus its finitely generated rational recession cone. Analogous statements with real generators hold for real polyhedra.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A convex combination with more than $a+1$ positive coefficients has an affine dependency $\sum b_jz_j=0$, $\sum b_j=0$. It has both coefficient signs. Subtract $t b_j$ from the coefficients, where $t=\min_{b_j>0}\alpha_j/b_j$. Nonnegativity, total coefficient and represented point are preserved, and at least one coefficient vanishes. Iteration leaves at most $a+1$ points. The hull is therefore the image of $K^{a+1}$ times the compact standard simplex under the continuous sum map, allowing repetition. This proves compactness, rather than assuming that a compact set's hull is closed.

For a conic representation eliminate a nonzero dependency among active columns in the same way, after choosing its sign to have a positive entry. The surviving columns are independent. On any independent subset its inverse column map bounds all coefficients by a constant times the represented vector's norm. There are finitely many subsets. A convergent sequence thus has a subsequence represented on one subset with bounded convergent coefficients. The limit belongs to the cone.

For finite inequalities, lineality is their joint homogeneous kernel and has a rational basis in the rational case. In the pointed quotient the sum of the defining nonnegative linear forms is strictly positive on nonzero cone vectors. Its section at one is bounded: otherwise normalized vectors have a unit limit with zero sum, hence in the removed kernel. A bounded polyhedron is its vertex hull. Indeed from a point that is not a vertex move in both directions in the kernel of its active constraints to endpoints where more independent constraints are active. Boundedness supplies the endpoints. Recursing terminates in at most the dimension many increases of active rank. Only finitely many active subsets occur, so there are finitely many vertices; rational full-rank systems give rational vertices.

Apply cone generation to the homogenized inequalities $(x,t)$, $t\geq0$. For a pointed polyhedron this cone is pointed; its positive-$t$ generators normalize to vertices, and its zero-$t$ generators give recession rays. At $t=1$ their coefficients yield a vertex convex combination plus a recession combination. This proves the stated decomposition. For real inequalities the same rank and compactness arguments apply over $\R$. Pure cone representations and linear images of finite cones/hulls are again finitely generated. They are also finite polyhedra: eliminate representation variables from their finite inequalities by pairing the upper and lower bounds on each variable (Fourier--Motzkin elimination). Positive, negative and zero coefficients give a finite list at each step, including equality elimination. This proves the needed finite-inequality description without a polyhedral duality citation.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nearest-point separation and supporting planes]\label{lem:support}
A point $z$ outside a nonempty closed convex set $C$ has a nearest point $w$ and the inward normal $n=w-z$ satisfies
\[
 n\cdot(c-z)\geq\|n\|^2>0\quad(c\in C).
\]
A finite continuous convex function on an entire real vector space has an affine support at every point and equals its biconjugate.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A minimizing sequence for distance may be restricted to a closed bounded ball, so compactness attains its minimum. Differentiate the squared distance along the segment from $w$ to $c$: $n\cdot(c-w)\geq0$, which gives the displayed strict separation.

For $F$ and $z_\epsilon=(x,F(x)-\epsilon)$, its closed convex epigraph has distance at most $\epsilon$; nearest points converge to $(x,F(x))$. Take a convergent subsequence of inward unit normals. Its limit $(a,\beta)$ is a unit supporting normal at this graph point and $\beta\geq0$, because the epigraph is upward closed. If $\beta=0$, projection of the epigraph onto the whole space forces $a\cdot(y-x)\geq0$ for every $y$, hence $a=0$, contradicting unit length. Dividing by $\beta>0$ gives an affine minorant touching at $x$. For $F^*(p)=\sup_y(p(y)-F(y))$, that support gives $F^*(p)=p(x)-F(x)$. Thus $F^{**}(x)\geq F(x)$; the defining inequality gives the reverse bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Full convex local Lipschitzness and derivatives]\label{lem:derivative}
A finite convex function on an entire finite-dimensional real space is locally Lipschitz and continuous. At a point $y$ its directional derivative
\[
 h(w)=\lim_{t\downarrow0}\frac{F(y+tw)-F(y)}t
\]
is finite continuous sublinear. Its subdifferential $K=\partial F(y)$ is nonempty compact convex and $h(w)=\max_{\ell\in K}\ell(w)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
On a cube around $y$, convexity bounds the function above by the maximum of its finitely many vertex values. Reflect a point of the half-sized cube through $y$ into the larger cube; the midpoint inequality gives a finite lower bound. On a smaller ball the absolute values are therefore bounded by a finite $M$. For two points in a concentric ball of half the radius, extend the ray from the first through the second by at least a fixed radius inside the larger ball. Convex secants bound the positive difference quotient by $2M$ divided by that radius. Reverse the points for the negative bound. This is local Lipschitzness and implies continuity; no continuity theorem for printed PC is being used.

Secant monotonicity gives the derivative limit; the Lipschitz bound gives $|h(w)|\leq L\|w\|$ and $|h(v)-h(w)|\leq L\|v-w\|$. Rescaling proves positive homogeneity. The inequality
$F(y+t(v+w))\leq\tfrac12F(y+2tv)+\tfrac12F(y+2tw)$ proves subadditivity after subtraction and passage to the limit. Thus $h$ is continuous convex sublinear.

Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:support} to $h$ at any $w$. If its touching affine support is $\ell(z)+b$, testing $z=0$ gives $b\leq0$; testing $z=2w$ and using equality at $w$ gives $b\geq0$. Hence $b=0$, $\ell\leq h$, and $\ell(w)=h(w)$. Forms below $h$ are exactly subgradients of $F$ at $y$: convex secants give $h(v)\leq F(y+v)-F(y)$; the opposite implication follows by differentiation of a support of $F$. Both signs of the norm bound make these forms bounded. Their defining inequalities make the set closed convex. It is nonempty by the support just constructed and compact by finite-dimensional compactness. This also proves its exact maximum representation in every direction.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Continuity on the whole compact polytope]\label{lem:boundary}
A finite lower semicontinuous convex function $a$ on a compact polytope $K$ is continuous on all of $K$, including every boundary point, in its relative topology. The polytope need not be rational.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Its upper bound is the largest of the finitely many vertex values, by the vertex-hull representation. Fix $x\in K$ and work in its affine span. Present $K$ by finitely many inequalities. Choose a positive radius smaller than every inactive inequality's slack at $x$ divided by its normal's norm. For nearby $z\in K$, the direction $v=(z-x)/\|z-x\|$ satisfies each active inequality homogeneously. Consequently $x+rv\in K$ for a single fixed sufficiently small $r>0$. With $t=\|z-x\|/r$,
\[
 a(z)\leq(1-t)a(x)+t\max_{\mathrm{vertices}\ v}a(v).
\]
This proves upper semicontinuity at $x$. Lower semicontinuity proves the other limiting inequality. The singleton case is immediate. Compactness then gives uniform continuity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closed compact-domain biconjugacy]\label{lem:biconj}
Extend a finite lower semicontinuous convex $a$ on a nonempty compact convex $K$ by $+\infty$ outside $K$. Then $a=a^{**}$ on the entire ambient space. In particular the assertion includes its boundary.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The epigraph is closed, convex, nonempty and upward closed. For $q\in K$ and $t<a(q)$, strictly separate $(q,t)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:support}. The normal $(A,\beta)$ has $\beta\geq0$. Evaluating the strict separation at $(q,a(q))$ gives
$\beta(a(q)-t)\geq\|n\|^2>0$, so \emph{strictly} $\beta>0$. Thus
\[
 a(s)\geq t+\|n\|^2/\beta-A\cdot(s-q)/\beta\quad(s\in K).
\]
This affine minorant has value greater than $t$ at $q$. It yields $a^{**}(q)>t$, and letting $t\uparrow a(q)$ gives equality with the elementary bound $a^{**}\leq a$. If $q\notin K$, spatial nearest-point separation gives a linear $u$ with $u(q)>\max_Ku$. Since $\min_Ka$ is finite,
\[
 (tu)(q)-a^*(tu)\geq t(u(q)-\max_Ku)+\min_Ka\longrightarrow+\infty.
\]
This proves equality outside the compact domain as well. A separator with possibly zero vertical component is insufficient for the within-domain argument; the displayed same-fiber test establishes positivity.
\end{proof}

\section{Continuous PC on dependent complete pages}\label{sec:criterion}
Translate a rational point of $H$ to zero and write $L_i=Q_i-Q_i$, $H$ now linear, and $E=\sum_iL_i$. Its rational lattice is saturated in $N$ and it is the actual ambient space used henceforth.

\begin{theorem}[Common ambient support criterion]\label{thm:criterion}
For a continuous real function $f$ on this book, printed PC is equivalent to ordinary convexity of every full-page $f_i=f|_{L_i}$ and the following condition: for every real $y\in H$ there is one $p_y\in E^*$ satisfying
\[
 f_i(x)\geq f_H(y)+p_y(x-y)\quad(x\in L_i,\ \text{all }i).                 \tag{CS}
\]
Agreement of convex traces alone is insufficient for dependent pages.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix an inherited rational carrier, refined to include $H$ if needed. Each convex cell is contained in one page: its relative interior cannot lie in a finite union of proper affine subspaces of its own affine hull. For example the product of nonzero affine equations for those subspaces is a nonzero polynomial and cannot vanish on an open set, as follows by repeated one-variable polynomial uniqueness. Thus each page receives an inherited rational subdivision; we do not discard its walls.

At any $x\in L_i$ let $\tau$ be its minimal center face. There is a neighborhood meeting only cells containing $\tau$, because all other finitely many closed cells avoid $x$. For all sufficiently small $u\in L_i$, choose assigned page cells $\sigma_+,\sigma_-$ containing $x+u,x-u$. The constraints
\[
 z\in\tau,\quad z+v\in\sigma_+,\quad z-v\in\sigma_-,\quad z,v\in L_i
\]
form a rational feasible polyhedron. Lemma~\ref{lem:density} approximates $(x,u)$ by rational feasible midpoint triples. Their Jensen inequalities and continuity give the local midpoint inequality at $x$ with the original incidences.

To get entire-page convexity restrict to any real segment, subtract its chord and call the resulting continuous function $q$ on $[0,1]$. If $q(t_0)>0$ internally, choose $\epsilon>0$ sufficiently small that $q(t)+\epsilon t^2$ has a maximum at some interior $t_*$. At nearby symmetric arguments the local midpoint inequality for $q$ implies
\[
 \tfrac12[(q+\epsilon t^2)(t_*+s)+(q+\epsilon t^2)(t_*-s)]
 \geq(q+\epsilon t^2)(t_*)+\epsilon s^2,
\]
contradicting maximality. Thus $q\leq0$ on every segment. This argument covers arbitrary inherited faces, including those in $H$, and never coarsens to a rational box.

At $y\in H$, Lemma~\ref{lem:derivative} supplies compact convex page subdifferentials $K_i=\partial f_i(y)$ and finite continuous sublinear $h_i(w)=\max_{K_i}\ell(w)$. Put $K=\prod_iK_i$ and $S=\operatorname{image}(E^*\to\prod_iL_i^*)$. The image is a closed linear subspace by finite-dimensional linear algebra, even when the pages overlap tangentially. If $K\cap S=\varnothing$, minimize $\|\operatorname{pr}_{S^\perp}z\|^2$ over compact $K$. Let $z_0$ minimize and $a=\operatorname{pr}_{S^\perp}z_0\ne0$. Differentiating on $[z_0,z]$ gives
\[
 \langle a,z\rangle\geq\|a\|^2\quad(z\in K).
\]
Identify the product dual with $\prod_iL_i$ using auxiliary inner products, and let $(w_i)$ represent $-a$. Orthogonality to $S$ means $\sum_iw_i=0$ in $E$. The product maximum is
\[
 \sum_ih_i(w_i)=\max_{(\ell_i)\in K}\sum_i\ell_i(w_i)
 \leq-\|a\|^2<0.
\]
For sufficiently small $t>0$, derivative convergence makes
$\sum_i[f_i(y+tw_i)-f_H(y)]<0$. These endpoints have equal-weight barycenter $y$. They lie in cells incident to its minimal face once $t$ is small. At real $y$ and real endpoints the perspective density proof in Lemma~\ref{lem:density} supplies exactly the same legal real-endpoint inequality. It forbids this negative cost. Hence $K$ meets $S$, and an ambient preimage gives (CS). Compact-product separation does not assume that restriction coordinates are independent; their entire compatibility is encoded by $S$.

Conversely, outside $H$ a center has a neighborhood in its unique page. Every incident endpoint cell is in that page: its segment to the center has an initial interval in the page; a cell contained in another page cannot have that interval. Ordinary Jensen applies. At a center $y\in H$, use the same $p_y$ in (CS) at every endpoint, assigning endpoints in $H$ to any page. Multiplying and summing cancels $p_y(\sum\alpha_j(x_j-y))$. This proves every printed PCC on every rational refinement. Diagnostic~\ref{sec:diagnostic} verifies the final insufficiency assertion.
\end{proof}

\section{Entire full-affine primal foundation}\label{sec:primal}
This section independently reconstructs the old289 full-affine reasoning, including exact finitely many contact samples. It is not an application of an unreviewed label.

Let $\gamma$ be convex finite real PA on a rational carrier of a complete rational affine space $L$, translated rationally to zero. Its affine top-cell expressions are global supports: on an interior point an affine derivative equals its convex supporting slope, and convex secants propagate it across the full space. Therefore
\[
 \gamma(x)=\max_j(a_j(x)+b_j),\qquad h(v)=\max_ja_j(v),\qquad\Delta=\conv\{a_j\}.
\]
All $a_j,b_j$ are the actual real data. The finitely many constants give a global bound on $\gamma-h$. On each recession cone of its rational carrier, $h$ equals the linear part of that cell. The cones cover $L$: along $tv$, $t\to\infty$, a finite carrier has a cell containing an unbounded subsequence; dividing its defining inequalities by $t$ puts $v$ in its recession cone. Taking its base point and its ray shows that the cell slope equals $h(v)$ by the bounded difference. Strict growth gives $h(v)>0$ for $v\ne0$, uniformly positive on the unit sphere. Thus $\Delta$ is a compact full-dimensional polytope containing zero in its interior. Otherwise separation supplies a nonzero direction on which its support function is nonpositive. Every carrying cell is pointed: a lineality $v$ would give $h(v)=a(v)$ and $h(-v)=-a(v)$, inconsistent with both being positive.

Choose the finite rational ray generators of these recession cones using Lemma~\ref{lem:hull}. They give the exact identity
\[
 C_h:=\epi h=\operatorname{cone}\{(r,h(r)),(0,1):r\text{ in the finite ray list}\}.            \tag{RC}
\]
On each cone, conic spatial combinations lift with equality because $h$ is linear there. Convexity and homogeneity of $h$ prove the converse inclusion. This is a real polyhedral epigraph with rational \emph{spatial} generators; irrational heights are allowed.

\begin{lemma}[Actual whole dual domain]\label{lem:dual}
For continuous convex $f$ with $|f-\gamma|$ globally bounded, $\phi=f^*$ has domain exactly $\Delta$ and is finite continuous on the entire actual polytope $\Delta$, including its boundary. Moreover $f(x+v)\leq f(x)+h(v)$, and $f$ has exact recession $h$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose $C$ with $|f-h|\leq C$. For $p\in\Delta$, $p(x)\leq h(x)$ gives $\phi(p)\leq C$; testing $x=0$ gives $\phi(p)\geq-f(0)$. For $p\notin\Delta$, strict separation gives $v$ with $p(v)>h(v)$, so $p(tv)-f(tv)\to+\infty$. Thus the domain is exactly $\Delta$. A supremum of continuous affine functions is convex lower semicontinuous, and Lemma~\ref{lem:boundary} gives whole-boundary continuity, even for an irrational polytope. Lemma~\ref{lem:support} gives $f=\phi^*$. Since $p(v)\leq h(v)$ on $\Delta$, this formula gives $f(x+v)\leq f(x)+h(v)$. Bounded difference and homogeneity imply the actual recession limit along every $x+tv$ is $h(v)$. One can also derive the recession inequality directly: an excess positive secant would force all subsequent secants above $h(v)$, contradicting bounded difference.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Global rational-spatial primal majorants]\label{thm:primal}
For every $\eta>0$ and every finite prescribed rational sample set $B\subset L$, there is a convex $F_A\in\PA_\R$ on a finite rational carrier, with exact recession $h$, such that
\[
 f\leq F_A\leq f+\eta\text{ on the whole }L,\qquad F_A(b)=f(b)\quad(b\in B).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Continuity gives $\phi(p)=\sup_{x\in L_\Q}(p(x)-f(x))$. At each $p\in\Delta$ choose a rational $x$ achieving error less than $\eta/2$. Continuity of $\phi$ makes its affine sample achieve error less than $\eta$ on a neighborhood in $\Delta$. Compactness selects finitely many samples $A$ covering \emph{all} of $\Delta$; append $B$ and one sample if necessary. Thus
\[
 \phi-\eta\leq g_A(p):=\max_{a\in A}(p(a)-f(a))\leq\phi\quad(p\in\Delta).
\]
Form
\[
 E_A=\conv\{(a,f(a)):a\in A\}+C_h.                                      \tag{PH}
\]
Its first term is compact and its cone is closed by Lemma~\ref{lem:hull}; a convergent sum has a convergent compact component and hence a cone limit, proving closedness. Its projection is the whole $L$. Convexity of $f$ and its recession inequality put $E_A\subset\epi f$. Every vertical fiber is a closed nonempty upward ray bounded below by $f$, so it has a finite minimum. Call the lower boundary $F_A$. It is finite convex, hence continuous by Lemma~\ref{lem:derivative}. Direct maximization of $p(x)-t$ on (PH) gives $F_A^*=g_A$ on $\Delta$; outside $\Delta$ a recession vector makes it infinite. Biconjugacy of finite continuous convex functions reverses the preceding bounds to give the stated global error. Each prescribed lift belongs to $E_A$ while $F_A\geq f$, proving exact sample values. The bounded global error implies unchanged actual recession and bounded difference from the original $\gamma$.

Lemma~\ref{lem:hull} also proves that (PH) has finitely many real affine inequalities and faces. Because its projection is all $L$, a nontrivial defining inequality cannot be purely spatial. Its lower facets therefore have positive vertical coefficient, and $F_A$ is the maximum of finitely many real affine forms. A lower face is generated by precisely the sampled vertices and cone generators on which its support attains equality; the upward ray is excluded. Its projection is a hull of rational positions plus a cone of rational directions, hence a rational polyhedron by rational elimination. Projection on a graph face is affine and injective. Intersections of graph faces project to common faces; face inclusions and intersections are preserved by this affine isomorphism. The finitely many projected lower faces and all their faces cover $L$ and form a genuine rational spatial complex. No irrational coefficient wall is declared rational. Lemma~\ref{lem:scalar} now gives primitive real scalar membership.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Rational matching gives real scalar membership]\label{lem:scalar}
A continuous real piecewise affine function on a finite rational carrier belongs to the real scalar extension of literal PA on that carrier.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Use rational affine origins and rational tangent bases on each cell. Equality of affine restrictions on shared faces is tested at finitely many rational affine basis points, giving a finite rational homogeneous linear system in the slopes and constants. Its real kernel has a rational basis by Gaussian elimination. Express the actual coefficient vector as a finite real combination of that basis. Each basis vector describes a continuous rational-affine function. A common positive integer multiple clears its finitely many slope denominators in the saturated cell bases, giving integral tangent slopes and rational constants; division by that integer is absorbed into its real scalar coefficient. This proves the assertion without rounding any actual slope.
\end{proof}

\section{The joint dual and simultaneous boundary fibers}\label{sec:joint}
Let $f$ satisfy Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} gives page convexity and common supports. Apply Section~\ref{sec:primal} separately to the actual page references. Write $h_i=\gamma_i^\infty$, $\Delta_i$ its actual gradient polytope, and $\phi_i=f_i^*$, finite continuous on all $\Delta_i$, infinite elsewhere. The trace $f_H$ is finite continuous convex. If $k>0$, $\gamma_H$ is convex finite real PA on its inherited rational carrier, with positive growth; its recession is $h_H=h_i|_H$ and its actual polytope is $\Delta_H$. Restrictions of the $\Delta_i$ to $H$ equal $\Delta_H$, because both have support function $h_H$, or by the separate fiber argument below. For $k=0$ use the singleton dual domain $\Delta_H=\{0\}$.

Let $r_i:E^*\to L_i^*$ and $T:E^*\to H^*$ be restriction. Define
\[
 D=\{p:r_ip\in\Delta_i\text{ for every }i\},\qquad
 \phi(p)=\max_i\phi_i(r_ip)\quad(p\in D),
\]
extending $\phi$ by infinity elsewhere.

\begin{lemma}[Joint compact dual and exact trace]\label{lem:joint}
$D$ is a nonempty compact polytope, possibly irrational; $\phi$ is finite continuous convex on it. For $G=\phi^*$,
\[
 G|_{L_i}\leq f_i,\qquad G|_H=f_H.
\]
For $q\in T(D)$, the compact fiber minimum $k(q)=\min_{Tp=q}\phi(p)$ is attained, convex and lower semicontinuous, continuous on the whole compact polytope $T(D)$. Extended by infinity, it satisfies
\[
 k^*=f_H,\qquad k=f_H^*,\qquad T(D)=\Delta_H.                           \tag{JF}
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each $\Delta_i$ has finitely many real inequalities, so $D$ does too. It is closed. If it were unbounded, normalized unbounded $p_n$ have a unit subsequential limit $p$. Bounded restrictions imply $r_ip=0$ for every $i$. Since $E=\sum_iL_i$, this forces $p=0$, a contradiction. Thus $D$ is compact and a polytope by Lemma~\ref{lem:hull}. A common support at any $y\in H$ has Fenchel equality on every page:
\[
 \phi_i(r_ip_y)=p_y(y)-f_H(y),
\]
so its restrictions lie in every $\Delta_i$; $D$ is nonempty. Composition and finite maxima give continuity and convexity of $\phi$ on $D$. We do not infer rationality of $D$ from rationality of the spatial geometry.

For $x\in L_i$, $\phi(p)\geq\phi_i(r_ip)$ implies
$G(x)\leq\sup_{a\in\Delta_i}(a(x)-\phi_i(a))=f_i(x)$. At $y\in H$ the common support above makes all page conjugate values equal, so $p_y(y)-\phi(p_y)=f_H(y)$. Hence $G|_H=f_H$. This does not say that $G$ extends $f$ elsewhere on $X$.

Every $T$-fiber in $D$ is compact nonempty over $T(D)$, so it attains its minimum. Mixing minimizers proves convexity. For $q_n\to q$ take minimizers on a subsequence realizing the lower limit; compactness gives a convergent subsequence with $Tp=q$, and lower semicontinuity gives $k(q)\leq\liminf k(q_n)$. The domain is a compact polytope, so its extension is closed and Lemma~\ref{lem:boundary} gives entire boundary continuity. Directly grouping a supremum over these fibers gives $k^*=G|_H=f_H$. Lemma~\ref{lem:biconj}, including its strictly positive vertical separator and spatial outside-domain argument, gives $k=k^{**}=f_H^*$. The actual trace conjugate has domain exactly $\Delta_H$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:dual}; consequently $T(D)=\Delta_H$, including every boundary fiber. In dimension zero these are the same compact scalar identities.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Simultaneous, not unrelated, minimizers]\label{lem:simultaneous}
The separate page fiber minimum over $\Delta_i$ at any $q\in\Delta_H$ equals $k(q)$. Every joint minimizing $p\in D$ with $Tp=q$ consequently satisfies
\[
 \phi_i(r_ip)=k(q)\quad\text{for all }i.                               \tag{SM}
\]
The set $M=\{p\in D:\phi(p)=k(Tp)\}$ is compact and surjects onto the whole $\Delta_H$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For a single page repeat compact fiber attainment, mixing, subsequential lower semicontinuity and biconjugacy from Lemma~\ref{lem:joint} with its restriction map $U_i:L_i^*\to H^*$. Its projected minimum $k_i$ has conjugate $k_i^*=f_i|_H=f_H$. Thus $k_i=f_H^*=k$, including equality of entire domains. Hence for every $p\in D$, each $\phi_i(r_ip)\geq k(Tp)$. At a joint minimizer their maximum equals $k(q)$, so every one is also at most $k(q)$; this proves (SM). Whole-domain continuity of $\phi$ and $k$ makes $M$ closed in compact $D$, and compact fiber attainment supplies a point of $M$ over every $q$. Tangent overlaps do not change this maximum-versus-common-lower-bound argument. Choosing separate minimizers without a common ambient preimage would not prove (SM).
\end{proof}

\section{Simultaneous trace lowering with preserved supports}\label{sec:lowering}
\begin{theorem}[Entire trace extension]\label{thm:extension}
Let $f_H\leq g\leq f_H+\eta$ be any finite convex function on $H$ with the exact trace recession, $\eta>0$. There are finite continuous convex functions $F_i$ on the entire pages with
\[
 f_i\leq F_i\leq f_i+\eta,\qquad F_i|_H=g,
\]
exact page recession $h_i$, and at every $y\in H$ one common ambient form supporting all $F_i$. These intermediate functions need not be PA and receive no MA.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $b=g^*$. Conjugating the two bounds and applying Lemmas~\ref{lem:dual} and \ref{lem:boundary} gives its entire domain $\Delta_H$, whole continuity there, and
\[
 k-\eta\leq b\leq k.
\]
For each page use the continuous compact image
\[
 S_i=\{(r_ip,b(Tp)):p\in M\},\quad
 E_i=\conv\bigl(\operatorname{graph}\phi_i\cup S_i\bigr)+\R_{\geq0}(0,1).
\]
The graph and $S_i$ are compact. Lemma~\ref{lem:hull}'s finite affine-dependence elimination proves their hull compact, so adding the closed vertical ray gives a closed set. It is convex, upward closed and projects onto all $\Delta_i$. The source heights are bounded below and include the graph of $\phi_i$, so its fibers have finite attained lower values $\psi_i$. This lower boundary is closed convex and finite on the whole $\Delta_i$; whole continuity follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:boundary}.

At a graph source the height is $\phi_i$; at a source from $M$, (SM) gives $\phi_i(r_ip)=k(Tp)$ and $b(Tp)\geq\phi_i(r_ip)-\eta$. Convexity of $\epi(\phi_i-\eta)$ therefore gives
\[
 \phi_i-\eta\leq\psi_i\leq\phi_i.
\]
Every graph source also has height $\phi_i(a)\geq k(U_ia)\geq b(U_ia)$, and each added source has equality with $b$. Since $b\circ U_i$ is convex on $\Delta_i$, all of $E_i$ lies above its epigraph lower bound. Thus $\psi_i\geq b\circ U_i$. For every $q$ choose one joint minimizer $p\in M$ over it. The added source yields
\[
 \psi_i(r_ip)=b(q)\quad\text{for all }i,\qquad
 \min_{U_ia=q}\psi_i(a)=b(q).                                          \tag{LF}
\]
These are attained identities, including boundary $q$.

Set $F_i=\psi_i^*$. Compact dual domains with bounded values make these finite convex functions; Lemma~\ref{lem:derivative} gives continuity. Conjugating the bounds gives the claimed global error. The exact dual domain and bounded values give recession support function $h_i$. Grouping the supremum by the attained fibers in (LF) gives $F_i|_H=b^*=g$ by finite convex biconjugacy.

For any real $y\in H$, continuity of $b$ on compact $\Delta_H$ attains
$g(y)=q(y)-b(q)$. Choose \emph{one} joint $p\in M$ with $Tp=q$. By (LF), for every page and every $x\in L_i$,
\[
 F_i(x)\geq (r_ip)(x)-\psi_i(r_ip)=p(x)-b(q)=g(y)+p(x-y).
\]
The same ambient $p$ supports all pages at $y$. Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} proves PC of their continuous gluing. The construction allows arbitrary continuous nonseparable convex trace data and uses the one shared $H$ throughout.
\end{proof}

\section{Finite rational PA trace extension and global decrease}\label{sec:regularization}
\begin{lemma}[Whole finite-PA trace epigraph]\label{lem:traceepi}
If $g$ above is finite convex real PA on a finite rational carrier of $H$, its entire epigraph is the hull of finitely many lifted rational spatial vertices $(v,g(v))$ plus the exact cone $\epi h_H$. All lifted heights may be real.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $k>0$ its strictly positive recession makes every carrying cell pointed by the lineality contradiction used in Section~\ref{sec:primal}. Alternatively a rational orthant refinement makes cells pointed. Lemma~\ref{lem:hull} writes each such cell as its rational vertex hull plus its rational recession cone. On a cell $g$ is affine, and its ray heights are exactly $h_H(r)$ by bounded difference. Hence every point of its epigraph is a convex combination of its cell's lifted vertices plus its lifted rays and a vertical vector. Take the finite union of all vertices. The reverse inclusion follows from convexity of $g$ and its recession inequality, so the whole identity follows. For $k=0$ there is one lift and the vertical ray. This is an entire epigraph assertion, not interpolation at a finite set alone.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Genuine rational PA page extension]\label{prop:PAextension}
If $g$ is as in Lemma~\ref{lem:traceepi}, there are page majorants $Q_i\in\PA_\R$, convex on finite rational carriers, with
\[
 f_i\leq Q_i\leq f_i+2\eta\text{ globally},\quad Q_i|_H=g,
\]
unchanged actual page recession, and every common support supplied by Theorem~\ref{thm:extension} remains a common support. Their gluing is genuine PA PC on a common finite rational complex.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:primal} to each intermediate $F_i$ with error $\eta$, appending \emph{all} the finitely many trace vertices as samples. This is legitimate because $F_i$ is convex, continuous, and globally boundedly relative to the unchanged actual $\gamma_i$. The resulting epigraph contains all lifts $(v,F_i(v))=(v,g(v))$ and the exact branch recession epigraph. The latter restricts to $\epi h_H$ on $H$. Lemma~\ref{lem:traceepi} therefore puts the whole $\epi g$ in the sample epigraph over $H$, giving $Q_i|_H\leq g$. Since $Q_i\geq F_i$ and $F_i|_H=g$, the reverse inequality holds on the \emph{whole} $H$. The global error is at most $2\eta$. The primal projected-face proof gives actual rational spatial carriers despite real heights, and Lemma~\ref{lem:scalar} proves real scalar membership.

Any affine common support of the $F_i$ at $y\in H$ lies below $Q_i\geq F_i$ and still touches because $Q_i(y)=g(y)$. Thus all of these common ambient supports survive at every real point of $H$, not just at sample vertices. Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} gives all PCC inequalities.

For completeness choose rational ambient hyperplanes whose intersection in $E$ is $H$. Cut every page carrier by them; $H$ becomes a union of faces. Take the finite rational induced complexes on $H$, together with its trace carrier, and their common hyperplane refinement. Every rational equation for an induced facet in $H$ extends to a rational ambient affine equation by a rational basis. Extend these cuts to all pages, and also extend the finite rational wall equations of each page carrier ambiently. The resulting finite arrangement refines each carrier and induces exactly a common rational refinement on $H$. Different pages can meet only in $H$, so their cut cells meet in common faces there; within each page the arrangement has the usual face property. This constructs one finite rational complex, without a change of values or irrational equality cuts.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}]
For $k>0$, use the full-affine primal theorem on $f_H$ relative to the actual $\gamma_H$ to obtain finite rational-spatial real PA convex $g_n$ with $f_H\leq g_n\leq f_H+\eta_n$, $\eta_n=2^{-n}$, $n\geq1$, exact trace recession. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:extension} and Proposition~\ref{prop:PAextension} with the same trace on all pages. They give a genuine glued $Q_n$ with $0\leq Q_n-f\leq2\eta_n$ globally, exact recession and common supports.

If $k=0$, take $g_n=f(0)$ exactly. The direct simpler construction applies Theorem~\ref{thm:primal} to each $f_i$, appending zero. Each majorant equals $f(0)$ there; every original common support survives by being below $f_i$. This gives error at most $\eta_n$, which in particular satisfies the same $2\eta_n$ bound. Thus the dependent common-origin bouquet case is immediate separately and does not need any normal independence.

Set $P_n=Q_n+4\eta_n$. The same constant on every page preserves trace matching and common supporting forms, and changes no recession. On all $X$,
\[
 4\eta_n\leq P_n-f\leq6\eta_n,\qquad
 P_{n+1}-f\leq6\eta_{n+1}=3\eta_n<4\eta_n\leq P_n-f.
\]
Hence $P_n$ is genuinely globally decreasing and globally uniformly convergent. If $\|f-\gamma\|\leq C$, all $n\geq1$ have relative norm at most $C+3$. No operator was used in this proof of the domain.

The cycle is positive balanced as proved in Section~\ref{sec:raw}. R230 now applies to every $u\in C_b(X)$ and the actual continuous PC reference. It gives $f=P_\gamma(u)\in C(X)\cap\PC$, $\|f-\gamma\|\leq\|u\|$. The preceding construction applies to that actual endpoint, without obstacle PA or separability assumptions. Only after this step may S272 assign its completed raw measure.
